Ok, so In regards to the comment made by @targos, I have tested this:

```js
describe("yes", function() {
  it("no", () => {});
  it("yes", () => {});

  describe("maybe", function() {
    it("no", () => {});
    it("yes", () => {});
  });
});

describe("no", function() {
  it("no", () => {});
  it("yes", () => {});

  describe("maybe", function() {
    it("no", () => {});
    it("yes", () => {});
  });
});
```

- `playwright test --grep "yes"`:  all ran besides no -> no, no -> maybe -> no
- `mocha --grep "yes"`:  all ran besides no -> no, no -> maybe -> no
- `jest --testNamePattern=yes`:  all ran besides no -> no, no -> maybe -> no

meaning `describe` can be used as a filter, but only to rule in its descendants - not to rule them out

Hello,

I'm implementing a [Node.js test runner integration in WebStorm](https://youtrack.jetbrains.com/issue/WEB-56064/).
I would like to save common developers workflows for the test runner integration.

One of these common workflows involves running a special test from a file that contains multiple tests.
This can be particularly useful when debugging a specific use case:

https://github.com/nodejs/node/assets/895560/fe7ac4ed-29e6-4400-80a7-cd1195994607

> [It's an example for `jest`](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/jest/package.json#L6)

To implement this workflow, it's essential to have the ability to configure a test runner to execute a specific test.
Unfortunately, I couldn't find a way to do this with the current `--test-name-pattern` implementation.

### Code Example 

Let's imagine that we have tests for callback and promise-based functionality (inspired by [`test-fs-write-file-flush.js` from the Node.js repository](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/e01c1d700d9fdb197ea40a444ebd228337a7dd6e/test/parallel/test-fs-write-file-flush.js#L52)):


```javascript
describe(`callback version`, () => {
  test(`valid`, () => new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    validatePositiveNumber(1, (error, isValid) => {
      try {
        assert.ok(isValid);
        resolve();
      } catch (e) {
        reject(e);
      }
    });
  }));

  // invalid, etc
});

describe(`promise based version`, () => {
  test(`valid`, () =>
    validatePositiveNumber(1)
      .then(isValid => {
        assert.ok(isValid);
      })
  );

  // invalid, etc
});
```

Let's attempt to filter the `valid` test case from the `callback version` suite,
using Node.js 20.4+ with the [merged 'test_runner: make --test-name-pattern recursive #48382' PR](https://github.com/nodejs/node/pull/48382).

- Filtering with `--test-name-pattern="callback version"` will run all tests in `callback version` suit ([example](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/node-js-test-runner-greater-or-equal-20_4/package.json#L7)).
- Filtering with `--test-name-pattern="valid"` will run all tests in the `callback version` and the `promise based version` suites because they contain `valid` test cases ([example](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/node-js-test-runner-greater-or-equal-20_4/package.json#L8)).
- Filtering with 2 `--test-name-pattern` parameters `--test-name-pattern="callback version"` and `--test-name-pattern="valid"` also runs all tests in this case. ([example](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/node-js-test-runner-greater-or-equal-20_4/package.json#L9)).
  However, for Node.js versions earlier than 20.4, this approach works with the code example ([example](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/node-js-test-runner-less-20_4/package.json#L7)).
- Filtering with `--test-name-pattern` and combined names as `^callback version valid$` doesn't run any test. ([example](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/node-js-test-runner-greater-or-equal-20_4/package.json#L6C63-L6C87)).

Can you please suggest a way to filter the `valid` test cases from the `callback version` suite in this code example?

### The example in other tests runners

I checked the example in other test runners, and here are the results:
- [AVA](https://github.com/avajs/ava) [doesn't support suites](https://github.com/avajs/ava/issues/222)
- [Jasmine](https://jasmine.github.io/) works well [with `--filter="^callback version valid$"` parameter](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/jasmine/package.json#L6), supports suites by [`describe()`](https://jasmine.github.io/api/edge/global.html#describe).
- [Jest](https://jestjs.io) works well [with `--testNamePattern="^callback version valid$"` parameter](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/jest/package.json#L6), supports suites by [`describe()`](https://jestjs.io/docs/api#describename-fn).
- [Mocha](https://mochajs.org/) works well [with `--grep="^callback version valid$"` parameter](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/mocha/package.json#L6), supports suites by [`describe()`](https://mochajs.org/#getting-started).
- [Playwright](https://playwright.dev/) works well [with `--grep="callback version valid"` parameter](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/playwright/package.json#L6), supports suites by [`describe()`](https://playwright.dev/docs/api/class-test#test-describe-1).
- [NODE TAP](https://node-tap.org/) works well [with `--grep="^callback version$" --grep="^valid$"` parameters](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/tap/package.json#L6), supports some kind of grouping for subtests. 
- [Tape](https://github.com/ljharb/tape) doesn't support [test filtration](https://github.com/ljharb/tape#command-line-flags) and [grouping](https://github.com/ljharb/tape/issues/273#issuecomment-198966201).
- [Vitest](https://vitest.dev/) works well [with `--testNamePattern="^ callback version valid$"`](https://github.com/DmitryMakhnev/tests-runners-filtration-cases/blob/a019aa06a39cc77d7dc81a8cc43f2d064157652f/vitest/package.json#L6), supports suites by [`describe()`](https://vitest.dev/api/#describe). 

### Suggestion to extend `--test-name-pattern`

So it appears that in general if some test runner supports grouping by `describe()`, the runner gives a way to filter a specific test through all levels of the groups. Perhaps we should consider extending the `--test-name-pattern` parameter to support this type of filtration more commonly.

After reviewing other test runners, it appears that we can simply extend the `--test-name-pattern` to support the full path to the test. For the code example provided, it would look like `^callback version valid$`. This solution allows us to address the issue without requiring any breaking changes and offers a straightforward and widely-adopted approach.   

Could please say what you think about it?
